1
00:00:00,980 --> 00:00:04,550
So for this session, we
will look into another more

2
00:00:04,550 --> 00:00:06,440
specialized application,
much like we

3
00:00:06,440 --> 00:00:09,330
did with reinforcement learning
in the morning, and plasticity.

4
00:00:09,330 --> 00:00:12,450
Now, we'll look into
decision making.

5
00:00:12,450 --> 00:00:15,200
And we will build the circuit
that actually does decision

6
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:19,130
making in a behavioral task.

7
00:00:19,130 --> 00:00:24,840
Now, a bit of some history notes
on the decision-making research,

8
00:00:24,840 --> 00:00:28,170
and particularly, we will deal
with perceptual decision making

9
00:00:28,170 --> 00:00:31,100
now with tasks that
have to do with evidence

10
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:36,390
accumulation over
time, and participants,

11
00:00:36,390 --> 00:00:38,870
be it humans or animals,
having to decide

12
00:00:38,870 --> 00:00:41,240
whether this perceptual
stimulus belongs

13
00:00:41,240 --> 00:00:43,400
to one category or another.

14
00:00:43,400 --> 00:00:45,240
Typically, there
are two categories.

15
00:00:45,240 --> 00:00:49,790
But you can have tasks with
multiple possible answers

16
00:00:49,790 --> 00:00:52,869
in any perceptual modality.

17
00:00:52,869 --> 00:00:57,680
So and the distinction between
these perceptual decision-making

18
00:00:57,680 --> 00:01:00,800
tasks and the previous tasks
that we saw category learning

19
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:03,930
is that with decision
making, and particularly

20
00:01:03,930 --> 00:01:06,060
the perceptual decision
making, the categories

21
00:01:06,060 --> 00:01:07,810
are known a priori.

22
00:01:07,810 --> 00:01:11,010
So participants don't need
to learn by feedback, which

23
00:01:11,010 --> 00:01:13,540
category is the correct one.

24
00:01:13,540 --> 00:01:18,090
They just need to tease
apart any ambiguity

25
00:01:18,090 --> 00:01:20,700
in the perceptual
stimuli by accumulating

26
00:01:20,700 --> 00:01:23,460
more and more evidence
over time to reaching

27
00:01:23,460 --> 00:01:25,980
a conclusion about
this stimulus seems

28
00:01:25,980 --> 00:01:30,180
to be of the category A or B.

29
00:01:30,180 --> 00:01:35,100
And I'd like to talk to you
about one family of models that

30
00:01:35,100 --> 00:01:40,110
essentially started the boom in
modeling decision-making tasks,

31
00:01:40,110 --> 00:01:42,450
or perceptual decision making.

32
00:01:42,450 --> 00:01:44,855
That is the
drift-diffusion family.

33
00:01:44,855 --> 00:01:46,960
You might have heard
of models like that.

34
00:01:46,960 --> 00:01:54,910
So with drift-diffusion
models, to go over them.

35
00:01:54,910 --> 00:01:56,080
How do they look?

36
00:01:56,080 --> 00:01:58,920
What's the mechanisms
around them?

37
00:01:58,920 --> 00:02:00,910
And what are the
first principles that

38
00:02:00,910 --> 00:02:03,520
actually guided their creation?

39
00:02:03,520 --> 00:02:06,680
And then we'll also look into
the random dot motion task.

40
00:02:06,680 --> 00:02:10,509
This is a very popular
behavioral task,

41
00:02:10,509 --> 00:02:13,030
purely perceptual again.

42
00:02:13,030 --> 00:02:16,400
And then, we will also move
on to the second model.

43
00:02:16,400 --> 00:02:19,660
That's the same model
that we will be building.

44
00:02:19,660 --> 00:02:20,990
It's quite a popular model.

45
00:02:20,990 --> 00:02:27,200
That's why we picked this one
from this one paper at 2002.

46
00:02:27,200 --> 00:02:31,300
It can actually capture the
evidence accumulation dynamics

47
00:02:31,300 --> 00:02:35,060
that the behavioral
DDM exhibits.

48
00:02:35,060 --> 00:02:38,500
And it has some excitatory
population and one inhibitor

49
00:02:38,500 --> 00:02:39,380
population.

50
00:02:39,380 --> 00:02:42,700
And as you will see,
it also has a flavor

51
00:02:42,700 --> 00:02:47,420
of this winner-take-all motif.

52
00:02:47,420 --> 00:02:51,340
But in this case, the
structure is more--

53
00:02:51,340 --> 00:02:54,180
it's more of an
abstraction over biology.

54
00:02:54,180 --> 00:02:57,020
This network is not very
biologically realistic,

55
00:02:57,020 --> 00:03:00,870
but it just gives us some
nice properties, because it

56
00:03:00,870 --> 00:03:02,500
captures behavioral results.

57
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:06,730
And it can also explicitly
model different receptors,

58
00:03:06,730 --> 00:03:09,120
as you will see.

59
00:03:09,120 --> 00:03:11,980
Now, starting with the
drift diffusion model,

60
00:03:11,980 --> 00:03:16,000
so as I was saying, there are
some principles behind it.

61
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:18,870
And you can find
mathematical derivations

62
00:03:18,870 --> 00:03:21,990
of it, or the mathematical
proof, for this trade off

63
00:03:21,990 --> 00:03:25,960
that I'm claiming here that
with the drift diffusion model,

64
00:03:25,960 --> 00:03:28,500
it can optimally
negotiate this trade off

65
00:03:28,500 --> 00:03:31,110
between accuracy and speed,
which means that if we ask

66
00:03:31,110 --> 00:03:34,420
the model to be of a
specific accuracy level,

67
00:03:34,420 --> 00:03:38,250
then we can tune it to
be the fastest model that

68
00:03:38,250 --> 00:03:39,910
reaches that accuracy level.

69
00:03:39,910 --> 00:03:42,840
By fastest, I mean, taking the
least amount of time gathering

70
00:03:42,840 --> 00:03:44,955
evidence before
making a decision.

71
00:03:44,955 --> 00:03:48,540
Or if you want a
model to respond

72
00:03:48,540 --> 00:03:51,030
at a fixed amount of
time, and likewise,

73
00:03:51,030 --> 00:03:53,100
a fixed amount of
evidence, then it

74
00:03:53,100 --> 00:03:55,830
can be the most accurate model.

75
00:03:55,830 --> 00:03:58,770
And essentially, the
model mechanism, you

76
00:03:58,770 --> 00:04:00,820
can see a graphic of this here.

77
00:04:00,820 --> 00:04:02,860
There's always
some starting point

78
00:04:02,860 --> 00:04:05,040
in between two decision bounds.

79
00:04:05,040 --> 00:04:07,480
And we have this
process that is, well,

80
00:04:07,480 --> 00:04:11,440
the combination of a drift
process with some tendency

81
00:04:11,440 --> 00:04:14,170
to go either up or down,
and some diffusion,

82
00:04:14,170 --> 00:04:16,466
some additive noise
on top of that.

83
00:04:16,466 --> 00:04:21,070
So you can think of it as a
particle moving across space

84
00:04:21,070 --> 00:04:23,840
until it reaches one of
the two bounds, right?

85
00:04:23,840 --> 00:04:28,380
And whichever bound it reaches,
then it makes that decision.

86
00:04:28,380 --> 00:04:31,550
So this is the case of a
binary decision-making task.

87
00:04:31,550 --> 00:04:33,880
So let's say that the
upper bound is left choice

88
00:04:33,880 --> 00:04:36,610
and the lower is right choice.

89
00:04:36,610 --> 00:04:39,880
And you can see colored, here
are all the free parameters

90
00:04:39,880 --> 00:04:40,995
that we can use.

91
00:04:40,995 --> 00:04:43,150
Starting point can
represent any bias

92
00:04:43,150 --> 00:04:47,020
that the participant might
have any tendency towards one

93
00:04:47,020 --> 00:04:48,260
category or the other.

94
00:04:48,260 --> 00:04:49,840
And this is
particularly useful when

95
00:04:49,840 --> 00:04:52,810
you vary the reward
that they might get

96
00:04:52,810 --> 00:04:55,720
from one choice over the other.

97
00:04:55,720 --> 00:04:59,950
Drift is the rate by which
they accumulate evidence.

98
00:04:59,950 --> 00:05:03,290
The bound separation, that's
another free variable that

99
00:05:03,290 --> 00:05:05,750
represents the
amount of information

100
00:05:05,750 --> 00:05:09,875
that a person or an animal
needs to make a decision.

101
00:05:09,875 --> 00:05:11,700
But there's also the
non-decision time.

102
00:05:11,700 --> 00:05:14,600
And here, we can account for
any amount of the response

103
00:05:14,600 --> 00:05:20,045
time that is not the participant
thinking about what they choose.

104
00:05:20,045 --> 00:05:23,100
The typical explanation for
this is motor execution, right?

105
00:05:23,100 --> 00:05:25,508
So because we make a
decision in our minds,

106
00:05:25,508 --> 00:05:27,800
but then it might take some
few hundred of milliseconds

107
00:05:27,800 --> 00:05:30,890
to actually execute
that decision.

108
00:05:30,890 --> 00:05:32,610
So we can account for
that time as well,

109
00:05:32,610 --> 00:05:37,550
and tease it apart from
the actual response time.

110
00:05:37,550 --> 00:05:40,490
And now, moving on
to the experiment

111
00:05:40,490 --> 00:05:45,260
that you will model in
a sense with a circuit,

112
00:05:45,260 --> 00:05:48,620
it's the random dot motion task,
where essentially, a participant

113
00:05:48,620 --> 00:05:52,430
sees this pool of
dots as they move

114
00:05:52,430 --> 00:05:55,070
in space inside this aperture.

115
00:05:55,070 --> 00:05:57,080
And they need to
decide essentially

116
00:05:57,080 --> 00:05:59,970
what direction the dots
move towards, right?

117
00:05:59,970 --> 00:06:01,980
Again, binary choices.

118
00:06:01,980 --> 00:06:05,610
The dots move either on average
towards the left or the right.

119
00:06:05,610 --> 00:06:09,480
And as the experimenter,
you can vary

120
00:06:09,480 --> 00:06:11,640
the level of coherence
between dots, which

121
00:06:11,640 --> 00:06:17,085
goes from 0% coherence, so all
dots move randomly, to some,

122
00:06:17,085 --> 00:06:20,250
let's say 50% value, where as
you can see, half of the dots

123
00:06:20,250 --> 00:06:22,960
move towards the
correct direction.

124
00:06:22,960 --> 00:06:23,770
That is right.

125
00:06:23,770 --> 00:06:27,130
And then 100% coherence is,
yeah, this is very clear.

126
00:06:27,130 --> 00:06:30,540
So and this is a very typical
mechanism in perceptual

127
00:06:30,540 --> 00:06:34,170
decision-making task, because
if the reward feedback is always

128
00:06:34,170 --> 00:06:37,107
the same, then the
talents comes from--

129
00:06:37,107 --> 00:06:38,440
and there is no learning, right?

130
00:06:38,440 --> 00:06:41,440
The talents comes from telling
apart perceptual stimuli.

131
00:06:41,440 --> 00:06:43,560
So you play around
with this ambiguity

132
00:06:43,560 --> 00:06:46,720
and you can see
how participants,

133
00:06:46,720 --> 00:06:49,740
be it animals or
humans, actually resolve

134
00:06:49,740 --> 00:06:51,275
this ambiguity over time.

135
00:06:53,880 --> 00:06:57,660
Moving on to the actual
model that you will work on

136
00:06:57,660 --> 00:06:58,840
with the exercises.

137
00:06:58,840 --> 00:07:01,570
So this is a schematic
of the model.

138
00:07:01,570 --> 00:07:04,880
Essentially, there are two
excitatory populations,

139
00:07:04,880 --> 00:07:10,000
A and B. Each one is
selective for one category.

140
00:07:10,000 --> 00:07:15,040
But there is also some
non-selective neurons here,

141
00:07:15,040 --> 00:07:17,590
also excitatory,
around this pool.

142
00:07:17,590 --> 00:07:21,520
But now, the difference is that
the two selective populations

143
00:07:21,520 --> 00:07:24,410
also receive specific
inputs to them,

144
00:07:24,410 --> 00:07:28,870
representing any input
from upstream regions that

145
00:07:28,870 --> 00:07:34,150
might signal one choice
being correct over the other.

146
00:07:34,150 --> 00:07:36,250
So this is essentially
visual stimuli coming

147
00:07:36,250 --> 00:07:39,040
in represented as IA or IB.

148
00:07:39,040 --> 00:07:42,620
And there's also the
interneuron population here.

149
00:07:42,620 --> 00:07:45,100
Of course, we always
need this feedback

150
00:07:45,100 --> 00:07:47,440
between some
excitatory population

151
00:07:47,440 --> 00:07:50,600
and some inhibitory ones,
as you saw in previous days.

152
00:07:50,600 --> 00:07:52,300
And the interneurons
actually project

153
00:07:52,300 --> 00:07:54,190
to both of these
populations, and

154
00:07:54,190 --> 00:07:58,090
to the non-selective
excitatory neurons in here.

155
00:07:58,090 --> 00:08:01,300
And on top of all this, there
is some background noise,

156
00:08:01,300 --> 00:08:03,600
or some background
spike trains, that

157
00:08:03,600 --> 00:08:06,940
represent upstream regions that
we're not explicitly modeling.

158
00:08:06,940 --> 00:08:09,840
We'll just model them as
Poisson spike trains coming

159
00:08:09,840 --> 00:08:13,575
into all of these neurons.

160
00:08:13,575 --> 00:08:15,690
So here, I also
include the numbers

161
00:08:15,690 --> 00:08:19,570
from the original model
from the Wang paper.

162
00:08:19,570 --> 00:08:23,820
We will use a reduced,
scaled-down essentially,

163
00:08:23,820 --> 00:08:25,028
version of this circuit.

164
00:08:25,028 --> 00:08:26,820
But as you can see,
the scaled-down version

165
00:08:26,820 --> 00:08:30,540
can still exhibit the
competition in one

166
00:08:30,540 --> 00:08:33,750
of the two populations winning,
either A or B, and a decision

167
00:08:33,750 --> 00:08:39,929
being made just by adding some
scaling factors to the model

168
00:08:39,929 --> 00:08:42,900
parameters.

169
00:08:42,900 --> 00:08:46,340
So and essentially,
as I was saying,

170
00:08:46,340 --> 00:08:49,680
there is a selective current
coming into each neuron, the IA

171
00:08:49,680 --> 00:08:50,610
and IB.

172
00:08:50,610 --> 00:08:55,170
And we can, as you can
see here, essentially,

173
00:08:55,170 --> 00:08:59,340
the two currents coming into
neurons for a level of coherence

174
00:08:59,340 --> 00:09:01,270
that is, well, that is zero.

175
00:09:01,270 --> 00:09:05,080
So this is why there is so
much overlap between the two

176
00:09:05,080 --> 00:09:05,980
currents.

177
00:09:05,980 --> 00:09:07,660
But yeah.

178
00:09:07,660 --> 00:09:10,460
For instance, for a
level of coherence of 6%,

179
00:09:10,460 --> 00:09:13,070
you can see the two currents
are actually split apart.

180
00:09:13,070 --> 00:09:15,790
And here, we'll see
the distribution

181
00:09:15,790 --> 00:09:19,570
of all potential current
values for IA and IB,

182
00:09:19,570 --> 00:09:22,220
because the current
coming in is stochastic.

183
00:09:22,220 --> 00:09:25,820
So we assume that it comes
from a normal distribution.

184
00:09:25,820 --> 00:09:30,288
And we essentially set both
the new value and the sigma,

185
00:09:30,288 --> 00:09:32,080
so both the mean and
the standard deviation

186
00:09:32,080 --> 00:09:33,620
of that normal distribution.

187
00:09:33,620 --> 00:09:37,240
So as you increase the
coherence more and more,

188
00:09:37,240 --> 00:09:41,260
those two normal distributions
will come further

189
00:09:41,260 --> 00:09:42,830
and further apart essentially.

190
00:09:42,830 --> 00:09:45,620
So the currents will be
more and more different.

191
00:09:45,620 --> 00:09:50,140
So decisions will
be easier to make.

192
00:09:50,140 --> 00:09:52,990
And I just wanted to show you
the model equations in order

193
00:09:52,990 --> 00:09:57,250
to highlight the fact that
this model explicitly includes

194
00:09:57,250 --> 00:09:58,940
receptor dynamics in it.

195
00:09:58,940 --> 00:10:04,090
So the first equation is just
the typical voltage gradient.

196
00:10:04,090 --> 00:10:07,615
And it includes this
synaptic current.

197
00:10:07,615 --> 00:10:10,800
And the synaptic current
has four terms in it.

198
00:10:10,800 --> 00:10:13,220
One of them is just
the external input

199
00:10:13,220 --> 00:10:15,380
that's coming in
that is representing

200
00:10:15,380 --> 00:10:16,950
the visual stimulus.

201
00:10:16,950 --> 00:10:19,470
So we assume that that
is an AMPA current.

202
00:10:19,470 --> 00:10:22,700
But there is also some
recurrent AMPA currents

203
00:10:22,700 --> 00:10:27,530
from other excitatory neurons
projecting to its neuron that

204
00:10:27,530 --> 00:10:31,040
are recurrent NMDA
currents, likewise,

205
00:10:31,040 --> 00:10:32,640
from other excitatory neurons.

206
00:10:32,640 --> 00:10:37,854
And there is also GABA currents
coming in from the interneurons.

207
00:10:37,854 --> 00:10:42,200
And you can see the
equation for each current.

208
00:10:42,200 --> 00:10:44,280
There are simpler
ones in most cases,

209
00:10:44,280 --> 00:10:47,630
except for the NMDA, which has
a bit more complicated dynamics.

210
00:10:47,630 --> 00:10:52,040
And also respectively,
you can see the gradients

211
00:10:52,040 --> 00:10:55,525
for the synaptic gates.

212
00:10:55,525 --> 00:10:58,050
So each neuron has
a voltage gradient.

213
00:10:58,050 --> 00:11:02,340
But it also has these synaptic
gradients modeling AMPA, NMDA,

214
00:11:02,340 --> 00:11:05,340
and GABA explicitly.

215
00:11:05,340 --> 00:11:07,000
So this is what I
was saying initially.

216
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:10,050
This model is not the most
biologically realistic

217
00:11:10,050 --> 00:11:12,670
because it assumes this
wall-to-wall connection.

218
00:11:12,670 --> 00:11:16,290
So the excitatory populations
are connected to one another

219
00:11:16,290 --> 00:11:20,050
in a dense manner, and within
the neurons in a dense manner.

220
00:11:20,050 --> 00:11:21,960
And you don't really
see this density

221
00:11:21,960 --> 00:11:24,520
at high density in
actual brain circuits,

222
00:11:24,520 --> 00:11:28,590
but it's a viable model because
it actually can actually

223
00:11:28,590 --> 00:11:32,280
highlight the importance
of receptor dynamics,

224
00:11:32,280 --> 00:11:35,760
and individual receptor dynamics
in the process of evidence

225
00:11:35,760 --> 00:11:38,250
accumulation and
decision making.

226
00:11:38,250 --> 00:11:40,510
And you will play around
with the receptor values.

227
00:11:40,510 --> 00:11:43,470
And you will see how the
decision-making behavior

228
00:11:43,470 --> 00:11:43,970
changes.

229
00:11:46,590 --> 00:11:49,950
So these are some more results
from the paper that you could

230
00:11:49,950 --> 00:11:51,640
replicate with the exercises.

231
00:11:51,640 --> 00:11:54,900
So on the right here, we see
different coherence levels

232
00:11:54,900 --> 00:11:59,010
starting from 50% going down
to an incoherent stimulus.

233
00:11:59,010 --> 00:12:03,640
But even in the incoherent
case of 0%, as you can see,

234
00:12:03,640 --> 00:12:06,950
these are populations,
say, A and B, right?

235
00:12:06,950 --> 00:12:11,040
Both populations begin
firing in a similar manner.

236
00:12:11,040 --> 00:12:14,900
But after a while, especially
this second dark line

237
00:12:14,900 --> 00:12:18,420
here, one of them will win over.

238
00:12:18,420 --> 00:12:19,400
So this one wins.

239
00:12:19,400 --> 00:12:22,220
And the other one
slowly fades away.

240
00:12:22,220 --> 00:12:24,980
A similar behavior to what
we saw before with winner

241
00:12:24,980 --> 00:12:29,600
takes all and with the
pink model as well.

242
00:12:29,600 --> 00:12:33,710
And this behavior is
even more pronounced

243
00:12:33,710 --> 00:12:35,580
with higher coherence, with--

244
00:12:35,580 --> 00:12:37,310
because with higher
coherence, it's

245
00:12:37,310 --> 00:12:40,680
clearer to see from early
on, which one is winning,

246
00:12:40,680 --> 00:12:44,340
right, because it also receives
a higher current to begin with.

247
00:12:44,340 --> 00:12:45,630
So yeah.

248
00:12:45,630 --> 00:12:48,050
Again, the same population
can win more easily

249
00:12:48,050 --> 00:12:51,540
with at 50% coherence
compared to 0%.

250
00:12:51,540 --> 00:12:54,530
And on the right here, we
see some essentially summary

251
00:12:54,530 --> 00:12:56,032
statistics of this behavior.

252
00:12:56,032 --> 00:12:57,740
You see some response
time distributions,

253
00:12:57,740 --> 00:13:01,830
which are always a useful
metric, especially when you have

254
00:13:01,830 --> 00:13:05,280
a perceptual decision-making
task, when the participants are

255
00:13:05,280 --> 00:13:07,350
free to respond.

256
00:13:07,350 --> 00:13:11,400
And you can also see some
more psychophysical curves

257
00:13:11,400 --> 00:13:16,900
when the accuracy varies
over coherence values.

258
00:13:16,900 --> 00:13:19,650
And yeah, this is
another typical result

259
00:13:19,650 --> 00:13:20,710
from behavioral studies.

260
00:13:20,710 --> 00:13:23,670
You can feed
sigmoidal curves on it

261
00:13:23,670 --> 00:13:28,620
and use their parameters to
maybe organize your participants

262
00:13:28,620 --> 00:13:32,250
into subpopulations, or to do
population-level statistics

263
00:13:32,250 --> 00:13:36,780
between, say, a control
group and any test

264
00:13:36,780 --> 00:13:39,480
group that you have.

265
00:13:39,480 --> 00:13:42,480
So yeah, this was the end of it.

266
00:13:42,480 --> 00:13:46,710
So feel free to
navigate from the course

267
00:13:46,710 --> 00:13:50,280
to the decision-making
section, and then open

268
00:13:50,280 --> 00:13:52,170
the decision-making notebook.

269
00:13:52,170 --> 00:13:54,840
And yeah, as always, feel
free to ask any questions

270
00:13:54,840 --> 00:13:55,920
that you might have.

271
00:13:55,920 --> 00:13:57,830
So thanks.

